Align the echo canceller anew when recording starts, and keep what it knows of the room

Patch 0009 made AEC3 anew each time the audio device started recording again: at a call's
start, and when a microphone turned off (the voice channel only receiving) is turned on
again. A new AEC3 knows nothing of the room. With a headset there is no echo, and AEC3 only
lets the microphone through untouched while the other side talks once its transparent mode
decides that there is none: the legacy classifier waits for 6 s of the other side's sound
without a converged filter (TransparentMode, strong_not_saturated_render_blocks_ over
6 * kNumBlocksPerSecond). Until then its suppressor treats the microphone as echo whenever
the other side's sound plays. Measured in the call lab with a headset and the other side
holding a tone: after each unmute the first words went out about 17 dB weaker and the
room's noise about 10 dB lower for 3 to 6 s, which then came back at once, as a step.

The audio processing now asks the echo controller to restart for recording
(EchoControl::RestartForRecording) before making it anew. AEC3 drops the played sound
queued while nothing was recorded, and at its next recorded block resets its render buffer
and delay estimator as a new AEC3 does at its first, and its echo remover as at a delay
change (kBufferFlush: filters, ERLE, initial state, suppressor's initial state), which is
all the alignment the stale state of 0009 was about; but whether there is an echo path at
all (transparent mode) is the room's, not the alignment's, and is kept
(EchoPathVariability::recording_restarted, AecState::HandleEchoPathChange). As a new
AEC3, the restarted one processes nothing before fresh played sound arrives
(render_properly_started_). Another echo controller still is made anew.

AudioProcessingImpl calls it with both its locks held (EchoCanceller3 checks its race
checkers). The request still comes after the audio device's StartRecording returns, so
that a recording that fails to start never restarts it (wrapper patch 0018's test); the
capture thread may process a frame or two before it, which the restart then realigns.

Every call after the first in a process keeps the AEC3 of the one before, realigned.

Measured in the call lab with wrapper patch 0019 (mic.sh unmute; 10 runs of each case
unless said, as medians with the range; "heard" is how long the other side's voice came
back to them above -50 dBFS):
- with a headset and the other side holding a tone (unmute:headset, 3 runs of three
  unmutes): the first word after an unmute went out at 0 dB against the later ones (-8 dB
  on average before, -17 at worst) and the room's noise in the first 0.5 s at 0 dB (-12
  before);
- a call's start with speakers, the other side talking: their voice came back at -49 dBFS
  at its loudest (-74 to -29) and was heard 0.9 s (0 to 6); with 0009, -56 dBFS (-81 to
  -46) and 0 s (0 to 2.5). Without render_properly_started_ the restarted AEC3 began from
  stale played sound: -36 dBFS (-70 to -25), heard 5.5 s (0 to 8.8);
- unmutes with speakers (20): heard 0.4 s (0 to 2.7), as with 0009 (0.5 s);
- the cost: a headset taken off for speakers while the microphone was off. The transparent
  mode kept says there is no echo, and the other side heard themselves after the unmute at
  -14 dBFS at the loudest (-20 to -12) for 6.4 s (2.3 to 14.5), where a new AEC3 let
  -31 dBFS (-50 to -19) through for 0.3 s (0 to 2.6). It happens only when the room
  changes while the microphone is off.

Tried and left out, each measured against the above in the same series:
- forcing an echo path change at the first delay found after the restart (to leave the
  transparent mode as soon as an echo is found): a call's start came back at -40 dBFS and
  was heard 3.5 s (6 runs), and the headset taken off was no shorter (5.3 s);
- the delay headroom of wrapper patch 0019 after a reset (AEC3 aligns from the audio
  device's delay with 2 blocks whatever its configuration): a call's start at -36 dBFS,
  heard 5.2 s (6 runs);
- legacy transparent mode taking a filter as sane within 5 blocks past that headroom rather
  than within 5 blocks: a call's start heard 2.2 s (0 to 6.2), and the headset taken off
  no shorter (5.5 s).

diff --git a/api/audio/echo_control.h b/api/audio/echo_control.h
index 9b28e82..cd90465 100644
--- a/api/audio/echo_control.h
+++ b/api/audio/echo_control.h
@@ -60,6 +60,14 @@ class EchoControl {
   // Returns wheter the signal is altered.
   virtual bool ActiveProcessing() const = 0;
 
+  // Fogata: the audio device starts recording again (a call, or a microphone
+  // turned on again) after played sound kept coming while nothing was
+  // recorded. The echo controller drops what it aligned of the played sound
+  // with the recorded one and finds that alignment again, as a new one would,
+  // but keeps what it learned of the room that does not depend on it. Returns
+  // false when it cannot; the caller then makes it anew.
+  virtual bool RestartForRecording() { return false; }
+
   virtual ~EchoControl() {}
 };
 
